    {
      "id": "eczema",
      "name": "Eczema",
      "aliases": [
        "paradoxical eczema",
        "drug-induced eczema"
      ],
      "bodySystem": "Skin Conditions",
      "ratingSchedule": [
        {
          "citation": "38 CFR 4.118",
          "note": "Schedule of ratings, skin.",
          "verified": false
        }
      ],
      "reviewNotes": [
        "SCOPE SPLIT 2026-09-28, and the former IDENTITY-DUPLICATION finding against atopic-dermatitis-eczema is RETRACTED as erroneous at the evidence level. This identity is NOT a synonym for atopic dermatitis and the two are deliberately NOT consolidated. This identity owns the generic and iatrogenic eczematous eruptions; its registered route is psoriasis -> eczema at POST_SURGICAL_OR_TREATMENT, which is PARADOXICAL ECZEMA INDUCED BY BIOLOGIC THERAPY FOR PSORIASIS (JAMA Dermatol. 2024;160(1):71-79, PMID 38055239, qualified-human verified 2026-08-19), whose own guard states that eczema must not be inferred from psoriasis itself and that exposure to the implicated biologic is required. Paradoxical eczema is a drug-induced eczematous eruption and is not atopic dermatitis. CROSS-REFERENCE: the atopic-disease route is owned by the sibling identity atopic-dermatitis-eczema in skin-expansion-wave-6.json, which carries the atopic-march edge to asthma; a reviewer must not merge the two, and a claim routed here must not be extended to atopic dermatitis or vice versa."
      ]
    },
